            <ArticleTitle>Nonatherosclerotic causes of coronary artery narrowing--Part I.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>509-12</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Approximately 5% of patients with acute myocardial infarction do not have atherosclerotic coronary artery disease but have other causes for their luminal narrowing. The first part of this three-part review of nonatherosclerotic causes of coronary narrowing focuses on congenital coronary artery anomalies, coronary fistula, and high take-off position of coronary ostia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
